From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dreyk <dreyksmail@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: leave GRO off on new network devices

GRO merges the received packets of a flow into larger ones before they
are forwarded. On a router whose Ethernet and Wi-Fi can neither
checksum nor segment in hardware (the MT7628 of the Archer C50 v4),
every merged packet is split and checksummed again in software before
it is sent on. Without GRO, forwarding on that router took 5-17 % less
CPU per Mbit/s, uploads from Wi-Fi were 10 % faster and WireGuard 7-8 %.

Register network devices with GRO off, so that every interface is
covered, also those created later (guest access points, a Wi-Fi uplink,
tunnels), without configuration. GRO stays a changeable feature:
netifd's device option gro or ethtool turns it on for a device, and
fraglist GRO (600-net-enable-fraglist-GRO-by-default.patch) is still
set for when it is.
---
--- a/net/core/dev.c
+++ b/net/core/dev.c
@@ -10578,10 +10578,10 @@ int register_netdevice(struct net_device
 	dev->ifindex = ret;
 
 	/* Transfer changeable features to wanted_features and enable
-	 * software offloads (GSO and GRO).
+	 * software offloads (GSO; GRO stays off until it is turned on).
 	 */
 	dev->hw_features |= (NETIF_F_SOFT_FEATURES | NETIF_F_SOFT_FEATURES_OFF);
-	dev->features |= NETIF_F_SOFT_FEATURES;
+	dev->features |= NETIF_F_SOFT_FEATURES & ~NETIF_F_GRO;
 
 	if (dev->udp_tunnel_nic_info) {
 		dev->features |= NETIF_F_RX_UDP_TUNNEL_PORT;
